.TH ntpdate 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
ntpdate \- get the time from a time server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpdate
[\fB\-s\fR]
[\fB\-d\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIport\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIhours\fR]
[\fB\-O\fR \fIseconds\fR]
.I server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ntpdate
asks
.I server
for the time and prints it.
.PP
It does
.B not
set the clock unless you say
.BR \-s .
On its own it is a query and nothing more.
.TP
.B \-s
Set the kernel clock to the time that comes back.  Nothing is printed
unless you also give
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B \-d
Print the time even when
.B \-s
is setting it.
.TP
.B \-p
Ask on
.I port
instead of 123.
.TP
.B \-o
Add
.I hours
to the answer, between \-12 and 12.  NTP speaks UTC and nothing in the
reply says where you are, so without this the clock is set to UTC.
.TP
.B \-O
Add
.I seconds
as well, for the half-hour and quarter-hour zones that
.B \-o
cannot express.
.SH OPTIONS COME FIRST
.B Every option must appear before the server name.
.PP
.B getopt
here is the System V one: it stops at the first argument that is not an
option, so everything after the server is an operand and is ignored.
.B "ntpdate host \-o 5"
is not an error \- it prints UTC, which looks like a perfectly good
answer and is five hours out.  GNU's
.B getopt
shuffles the arguments, so the same line works on a Linux box, which is
how this is usually discovered.  From this release it is refused rather
than ignored.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the clock to UK winter time and keep it over a power cut:
.nf

# ntpdate \-s time.cloudflare.com
# setdate \-w

.fi
British summer time, one hour on:
.nf

# ntpdate \-s \-o 1 time.cloudflare.com

.fi
India, five and a half hours on:
.nf

# ntpdate \-s \-o 5 \-O 1800 time.cloudflare.com

.fi
Just ask, and change nothing:
.nf

# ntpdate time.cloudflare.com
Thu Sep 17 09:36:22 2026

.fi
.SH NOTES
Do this before anything involving TLS.  A certificate is only valid
between two dates, so a machine that thinks it is 1970 rejects every
certificate on the internet \- and the error will not mention the clock.
.PP
The kernel clock is lost at power off.  Follow
.B \-s
with
.B setdate \-w
to write the time to the DS3231.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setdate (1),
.BR date (1),
.BR tlsca (1),
.BR wifi (1)
